    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thx for that. so if we look at the leucine threshold—is there a specific way that 'leucine resistance' is thought to work at the cellular level during a plateau? i'm curious if the sensitivity of the Sestrin sensors can actually degrade over time or if it's just a matter of the signaling pathway becoming desensitized due to chronic overstimulation.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Leucine resistance during a plateau is thought to involve altered sensitivity within the upstream regulators, rather than a complete degradation of Sestrin sensors. Chronic, excessive stimulation can lead to downstream desensitization or adaptations where the mTOR pathway becomes less responsive to the same level of leucine stimulus. This often involves changes in phosphorylation status or expression of rate-limiting components beyond just the nutrient sensor itself. Therefore, the disruption is usually a dynamic recalibration of pathway sensitivity rather than simple sensor failure.",
      "source": "target",
